1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:12,508
Back in episode two, we said that x86, ARM, and RISC-V are three different instruction sets.

2
00:00:12,508 --> 00:00:19,640
Give the same line of C to three compilers, and the machine code that comes out is completely different.

3
00:00:19,640 --> 00:00:23,700
Most phones use ARM chips, and most computers use x86.

4
00:00:23,700 --> 00:00:30,352
Even the lengths differ, since x86 instructions come in different sizes, and this one is only two bytes.

5
00:00:30,352 --> 00:00:34,893
RISC-V instructions are always thirty-two bits, as wide as a data word,

6
00:00:34,893 --> 00:00:41,366
so fetching an instruction and reading data can share one piece of memory hardware.

7
00:00:41,366 --> 00:00:45,152
So a program's binary is tied to its instruction set.

8
00:00:45,152 --> 00:00:49,651
A RISC-V executable simply cannot run on an Intel x86 computer.

9
00:00:49,651 --> 00:00:53,722
However, an instruction set is often backward compatible,

10
00:00:53,722 --> 00:01:03,166
and today's x86 processors can still run programs written in nineteen eighty-one for the Intel eight oh eight eight.

11
00:01:03,166 --> 00:01:09,872
The last episode translated assembly into machine code, and this episode goes the other way.

12
00:01:09,872 --> 00:01:13,881
Given machine code, how do we read it back as assembly?

13
00:01:13,881 --> 00:01:15,849
That is called disassembly.

14
00:01:15,849 --> 00:01:19,133
Let's practice on this word, 0x01B342B3.

15
00:01:19,133 --> 00:01:25,513
Step one is to convert to binary, where each hexadecimal digit expands into exactly four bits.

16
00:01:25,513 --> 00:01:31,419
Next we find the opcode, which, in every format, always occupies the lowest seven bits.

17
00:01:31,419 --> 00:01:39,033
Every format has its own set of opcodes, so we look it up, and zero one one zero zero one one is an R-type.

18
00:01:39,033 --> 00:01:39,729
Step two.

19
00:01:39,729 --> 00:01:45,221
Once we know the format, we know how to cut the other twenty-five bits.

20
00:01:45,221 --> 00:01:50,233
Cut as an R-type, they are funct7, rs2, rs1, funct3, and rd.

21
00:01:50,233 --> 00:01:51,040
Step three.

22
00:01:51,040 --> 00:01:58,524
The funct3 is one zero zero and the funct7 is all zeros, and looking up the R-type table, this is xor.

23
00:01:58,524 --> 00:02:07,400
Note that the mnemonic xor isn't stored in any one field, but is decided together by the opcode, funct3, and funct7.

24
00:02:07,400 --> 00:02:08,222
Step four.

25
00:02:08,222 --> 00:02:12,335
The register fields are five-bit unsigned numbers.

26
00:02:12,335 --> 00:02:19,904
So rd is zero zero one zero one, which is five, rs1 is zero zero one one zero, which is six,

27
00:02:19,904 --> 00:02:24,428
and rs2 is one one zero one one, which is twenty-seven.

28
00:02:24,428 --> 00:02:29,776
Converting to register names, x5 is t0, x6 is t1, and x27 is s11.

29
00:02:29,776 --> 00:02:33,200
Put together, we get xor t0, t1, s11.

30
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:35,515
Be careful about the order.

31
00:02:35,515 --> 00:02:46,900
In assembly we write rd, rs1, rs2, but the fields run from left to right as rs2, rs1, rd, which is exactly the reverse.

32
00:02:46,900 --> 00:02:50,134
That is all there is to disassembly, just four steps.

33
00:02:50,134 --> 00:02:56,366
The first two are the same for every format, and the last two depend on the format.

34
00:02:56,366 --> 00:03:03,745
Looking back at the full R-type table, ten instructions share one opcode, but funct3 has only eight values.

35
00:03:03,745 --> 00:03:11,124
Two pairs have the same funct3, add and sub are both zero zero zero, and srl and sra are both one zero one.

36
00:03:11,124 --> 00:03:19,000
The funct7 field tells them apart, and it also has only two values, which differ in just one bit, bit thirty.

37
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:24,500
Just telling the operations apart uses seventeen bits, which is clearly more than needed.

38
00:03:24,500 --> 00:03:29,566
But keeping the field positions aligned matters more than saving a few bits.

39
00:03:29,566 --> 00:03:32,265
Take add t0, t1, s11 as an example.

40
00:03:32,265 --> 00:03:37,278
Flip bit thirty from zero to one, and it becomes sub t0, t1, s11.

41
00:03:37,278 --> 00:03:42,599
Add and sub share one adder, and bit thirty is just a switch, a flag.

42
00:03:42,599 --> 00:03:50,833
When it is one, rs2 is first negated, by flipping its bits and adding one, and then the two are added.

43
00:03:50,833 --> 00:03:54,822
Change funct3 to one zero one, and it becomes a right shift.

44
00:03:54,822 --> 00:04:01,670
With bit thirty at zero it is srl, a logical right shift, which fills the vacated high bits with zeros.

45
00:04:01,670 --> 00:04:08,983
With bit thirty at one it becomes sra, an arithmetic right shift, which fills the high bits with the sign bit.

46
00:04:08,983 --> 00:04:12,333
This time the switch controls sign extension.

47
00:04:12,333 --> 00:04:17,316
Shift immediates use a variant of the I format, which CS61C calls I-star,

48
00:04:17,316 --> 00:04:24,166
where the shift amount is at most thirty-one and takes only the low five bits of the immediate.

49
00:04:24,166 --> 00:04:30,852
The upper seven bits of the immediate aren't used as a number, but as a switch, just like funct7.

50
00:04:30,852 --> 00:04:37,745
For srai those seven bits are zero one zero zero zero zero zero, and the switch is still bit thirty.

51
00:04:37,745 --> 00:04:40,295
Turn bit thirty off and you get srli.

52
00:04:40,295 --> 00:04:47,733
There is only one kind of left shift, the logical one, so for slli this bit is always zero.

53
00:04:47,733 --> 00:04:50,193
The I format is more than addi.

54
00:04:50,193 --> 00:04:59,559
Put the immediate operations next to the R-type, and funct3 matches one to one, so addi pairs with add, xori with xor,

55
00:04:59,559 --> 00:05:01,543
slti with slt, and so on.

56
00:05:01,543 --> 00:05:09,200
The only one missing is subi, since to subtract a constant, you just addi a negative number.

57
00:05:09,200 --> 00:05:17,109
The three-bit funct3 has only eight encodings, and all of them are used up here, so srli, along with srai,

58
00:05:17,109 --> 00:05:20,533
still has to be told apart by bit thirty.

59
00:05:20,533 --> 00:05:26,435
The five loads also need funct3 to tell them apart, so they get an opcode of their own.

60
00:05:26,435 --> 00:05:29,566
All told, the I format uses four opcodes.

61
00:05:29,566 --> 00:05:35,312
The ecall instruction, short for environment call, asks the operating system for a service,

62
00:05:35,312 --> 00:05:38,091
such as printing text or ending the program.

63
00:05:38,091 --> 00:05:46,333
The ebreak instruction hands control to the debugger, and that is how breakpoints in a debugger are implemented.

64
00:05:46,333 --> 00:05:48,317
Neither of these has operands.

65
00:05:48,317 --> 00:05:56,766
Ecall is all zeros apart from the opcode, and ebreak just has an extra one in the lowest bit of the immediate.

66
00:05:56,766 --> 00:06:01,114
The funct3 values for loads and stores are matched too.

67
00:06:01,114 --> 00:06:09,256
The pair lb and sb uses zero zero zero, lh and sh uses zero zero one, and lw and sw uses zero one zero.

68
00:06:09,256 --> 00:06:13,366
The lbu and lhu instructions have no matching store,

69
00:06:13,366 --> 00:06:22,966
because a store writes only the bytes it is given and involves no extension, and for the same reason RV32 has no lwu.

70
00:06:22,966 --> 00:06:26,120
Why do loads and jalr use the I format too?

71
00:06:26,120 --> 00:06:33,455
Both first compute rs1 plus the immediate, one to get an address and the other to get a jump target,

72
00:06:33,455 --> 00:06:37,033
so they can reuse the same adder.

73
00:06:37,033 --> 00:06:45,733
The jalr instruction first stores PC plus four into rd, and then jumps to rs1 plus the immediate.

74
00:06:45,733 --> 00:06:52,523
In episode seven, ret and jr ra were pseudo-instructions with no opcode of their own,

75
00:06:52,523 --> 00:06:56,837
and the assembler turns them both into jalr x0, ra, 0.

76
00:06:56,837 --> 00:07:02,669
The rd is x0 because on a return we don't need to leave a return address,

77
00:07:02,669 --> 00:07:07,333
and whatever is written into x0 is simply thrown away.

78
00:07:07,333 --> 00:07:13,341
For the encoding, the immediate is zero, rs1 is ra, which is x1, and rd is x0.

79
00:07:13,341 --> 00:07:21,660
There is only one jalr, so funct3 isn't really needed, and we fill in zero zero zero as the format requires.

80
00:07:21,660 --> 00:07:27,592
The opcode is one one zero zero one one one, and together this is 0x00008067.

81
00:07:27,592 --> 00:07:34,366
When you see it in a disassembly, you know a function is about to return.

82
00:07:34,366 --> 00:07:38,565
Here is another S-format example, sw x14, 36(x2).

83
00:07:38,565 --> 00:07:47,650
The number thirty-six in twelve-bit binary is zero zero zero zero, zero zero one zero, zero one zero zero.

84
00:07:47,650 --> 00:07:56,220
The upper seven bits, zero zero zero zero zero zero one, go into imm[11:5], and the lower five bits,

85
00:07:56,220 --> 00:08:00,200
zero zero one zero zero, go into imm[4:0].

86
00:08:00,200 --> 00:08:01,167
Why split it?

87
00:08:01,167 --> 00:08:08,310
If we used only the seven bits of funct7, we could represent just a hundred twenty-eight values.

88
00:08:08,310 --> 00:08:13,891
A store has no rd, so it borrows the five bits of rd to make up all twelve.

89
00:08:13,891 --> 00:08:20,364
Everything else stays as before, rs2 is x14, rs1 is x2, funct3 is zero one zero for sw,

90
00:08:20,364 --> 00:08:24,466
and the opcode is zero one zero zero zero one one.

91
00:08:24,466 --> 00:08:26,578
The result is 0x02E12223.

92
00:08:26,578 --> 00:08:35,534
Disassembling goes the other way, putting the two pieces back together, zero zero zero zero zero zero one,

93
00:08:35,534 --> 00:08:40,500
zero zero one zero zero, which is thirty-six.

94
00:08:40,500 --> 00:08:42,992
Finally, two questions to leave you with.

95
00:08:42,992 --> 00:08:45,425
Translate these two words into assembly.

96
00:08:45,425 --> 00:08:48,966
Pause the video and try it yourself.

97
00:08:48,966 --> 00:08:58,340
For question one, the opcode is zero one one zero zero one one, which is R-type, and funct3 and funct7 are all zero,

98
00:08:58,340 --> 00:08:59,390
so it is add.

99
00:08:59,390 --> 00:09:09,066
Here rd is one, rs1 is two, and rs2 is three, so the answer is add x1, x2, x3, which is add ra, sp, gp.

100
00:09:09,066 --> 00:09:17,837
For question two, the opcode zero zero zero zero zero one one is a load, and funct3 equal to zero one zero means lw.

101
00:09:17,837 --> 00:09:22,147
Then rd is ten, which is a0, and rs1 is two, which is sp.

102
00:09:22,147 --> 00:09:28,876
The immediate, one one one one, one one one one, one one zero zero, has a top bit of one,

103
00:09:28,876 --> 00:09:31,900
so in two's complement it is minus four.

104
00:09:31,900 --> 00:09:35,100
So the answer is lw a0, -4(sp).

